2.3 Formal System Definitions and Assumptions

Definition 2.7 (Settlement Parameters).
For a settlement of N permanent residents, define

Symbol Meaning Nominal range
m_h Habitat mass per person 325–375\ \text{t/person}
\eta ISRU utilisation fraction time‑varying; asymptotic \eta_{\max}\in[0.88,0.95]
f_p Productive fraction of landed payload 0.45–0.60
\varepsilon Overhead and loss factor 1.10–1.20
M_E Total Earth‑launched mass unknown; to be bounded
\eta_{\max} Maximum achievable ISRU fraction 0.90–0.95

Assumption 2.1 (Mass Conservation).
The total mass required to support N residents can be decomposed as

M_{\text{total}}(N) = M_{\text{imported}} + M_{\text{local}},

with no irreversible losses on the settlement timescale.

Assumption 2.2 (ISRU Separability).
A fraction \eta of the total mass requirement can be supplied by local ISRU production; the remaining fraction 1-\eta must be imported from Earth.

Assumption 2.3 (Payload Conversion Efficiency).
Only a fraction f_p of the Earth‑launched mass M_E constitutes usable payload after transit, entry/descent/landing, and packaging. The overhead factor \varepsilon \ge 1 accounts for consumable losses, spares, and non‑replicable components.

Assumption 2.4 (Quasi‑Static Approximation).
In the derivation of the static Bootstrap Inequality, \eta is treated as a time‑averaged constant over the build‑out phase. The dynamic ramp is treated separately.

Assumption 2.5 (Electronics Non‑Replication).
A mass fraction \delta \in [0.05,0.15] of total system mass (electronics, precision instruments) cannot be manufactured locally in the early phase; this is absorbed into \varepsilon as an additional import multiplier.

