2.4 Derivation of the Bootstrap Inequality

Theorem 2.1 (Bootstrap Inequality).
Under Assumptions 2.1–2.5, the Earth‑launched mass required to support N permanent residents at a steady ISRU utilisation \eta must satisfy

\boxed{M_E \ge \frac{N \cdot m_h \cdot (1-\eta) \cdot \varepsilon}{f_p}} \tag{2.1}

Equality holds only in the idealised limit f_p \to 1,\ \varepsilon \to 1.

Proof.
From Assumption 2.1 the total mass requirement is

M_{\text{total}}(N) = N \cdot m_h. \tag{2.2}

The locally produced mass is, by Assumption 2.2,

M_{\text{local}} = \eta \cdot N \cdot m_h. \tag{2.3}

Hence the required import mass is

M_{\text{imported}} = (1-\eta) \cdot N \cdot m_h. \tag{2.4}

Assumption 2.3 states that the usable payload delivered to the surface is M_E \cdot f_p, and that the gross import requirement is inflated by the overhead factor \varepsilon. Therefore the payload constraint is

M_E \cdot f_p \ge M_{\text{imported}} \cdot \varepsilon = (1-\eta) \cdot N \cdot m_h \cdot \varepsilon. \tag{2.5}

Rearranging yields (2.1). \square

Definition 2.8 (Bootstrap Constant).
Define

K \equiv \frac{m_h \cdot \varepsilon}{f_p}. \tag{2.6}

The inequality then takes the convenient linear form

M_E \ge K \cdot N \cdot (1-\eta). \tag{2.7}

For the nominal parameters (m_h = 325\ \text{t}, f_p = 0.50, \varepsilon = 1.15),

K = \frac{325 \times 1.15}{0.50} = 747.5\ \text{t/person}.

For the conservative set (m_h = 375\ \text{t}, same f_p,\varepsilon), K \approx 862.5\ \text{t/person}.

Corollary 2.1 (Sensitivity Analysis).
The partial derivatives of the lower bound with respect to the key parameters are

\frac{\partial M_E}{\partial \eta} = -K \cdot N, \qquad
\frac{\partial M_E}{\partial f_p} = -\frac{N \cdot m_h \cdot (1-\eta) \cdot \varepsilon}{f_p^2} < 0, \qquad
\frac{\partial M_E}{\partial \varepsilon} = \frac{N \cdot m_h \cdot (1-\eta)}{f_p} > 0.

Thus a one percentage point absolute increase in \eta (e.g., from 0.92 to 0.93) reduces the required Earth mass by K \cdot N \cdot 0.01 \approx 86\ \text{t} under the nominal parameters, roughly one full Starship payload.

Remark 2.1 (Category‑Weighted ISRU Fraction).
Assumption 2.2 treats \eta as a uniform scalar. In reality, shielding regolith is 100\% locally substitutable, structural steel \sim75\%, and electronics <10\%. A mass‑fraction‑weighted effective ISRU fraction is

\eta_{\text{eff}} = \sum_i w_i \eta_i, \qquad \sum_i w_i = 1,

where w_{\text{shield}} \approx 0.82, w_{\text{struct}} \approx 0.03, w_{\text{infra}} \approx 0.04. With \eta_{\text{shield}} = 1.0, \eta_{\text{struct}} = 0.75, \eta_{\text{infra}} = 0.20 one obtains \eta_{\text{eff}} \approx 0.865, slightly lower than the scalar \eta = 0.92 used in many MSAT simulations. This discrepancy is addressed in the uncertainty propagation of Chapter 6.

