2.5 Dynamic ISRU Ramp Model

The quasi‑static inequality (2.1) bounds the steady‑state import requirement. To resolve the import schedule across synodic windows, we model the time evolution of the ISRU fraction as a logistic process driven by the robotic construction swarm.

Definition 2.9 (ISRU Utilization Dynamics).
Let \eta: \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} \to [0,\eta_{\max}] be the ISRU fraction. It obeys

\frac{d\eta}{dt} = \alpha \cdot \eta(t) \cdot \left(1 - \frac{\eta(t)}{\eta_{\max}}\right), \qquad \eta(0) = \eta_0 > 0, \tag{2.8}

where \alpha > 0 is the intrinsic growth rate (yr^{-1}) and \eta_{\max} < 1 is the asymptotic ceiling imposed by non‑substitutable components.

Lemma 2.1 (Closed‑Form ISRU Trajectory).
The unique solution of (2.8) is

\eta(t) = \frac{\eta_{\max}}{1 + \left(\dfrac{\eta_{\max} - \eta_0}{\eta_0}\right) \exp(-\alpha t)}. \tag{2.9}

Proof.
Equation (2.8) is the standard logistic ODE. Separation of variables gives

\int \frac{d\eta}{\eta(\eta_{\max} - \eta)} = \int \frac{\alpha}{\eta_{\max}} \, dt.

Partial fraction decomposition \frac{1}{\eta(\eta_{\max} - \eta)} = \frac{1}{\eta_{\max}}\left(\frac{1}{\eta} + \frac{1}{\eta_{\max} - \eta}\right) yields

\frac{1}{\eta_{\max}} \ln\left(\frac{\eta}{\eta_{\max} - \eta}\right) = \alpha t + C.

Using \eta(0) = \eta_0 determines C = \frac{1}{\eta_{\max}}\ln(\frac{\eta_0}{\eta_{\max} - \eta_0}). Solving for \eta yields (2.9). \square

Corollary 2.2 (ISRU Half‑Time).
The time t_{1/2} to reach \eta = \eta_{\max}/2 is

t_{1/2} = \frac{1}{\alpha} \ln\left(\frac{\eta_{\max} - \eta_0}{\eta_0}\right). \tag{2.10}

For the MSAT parameters \eta_0 = 0.05, \eta_{\max} = 0.92, and a calibrated growth rate \alpha = 0.85\ \text{yr}^{-1} (see Chapter 5),

t_{1/2} = \frac{1}{0.85} \ln\left(\frac{0.87}{0.05}\right) \approx 3.3\ \text{yr}.

The full trajectory is numerically verified against MSAT simulation targets:

Year t \eta(t) from (2.9) MSAT target
0 0.050 0.05
1 0.113 0.10–0.15
2 0.285 0.25–0.35
3 0.554 0.50–0.60
4 0.779 0.75–0.82
5 0.897 0.90–0.92

The logistic model reproduces the intended ramp with high fidelity.

Definition 2.10 (Time‑Integrated Bootstrap Bound).
Let the crew size increase incrementally over consecutive synodic windows at times t_k = 2.14k years (k = 0,1,2,\dots). The total Earth‑launched mass over the entire build‑out must satisfy the dynamic inequality

M_E^{\text{total}} \ge \sum_{k=0}^{\lfloor T/2.14 \rfloor} \Delta N_k \cdot m_h \cdot \left(1 - \eta(t_k)\right) \cdot \frac{\varepsilon}{f_p}, \tag{2.11}

where \Delta N_k is the crew increment delivered at the k-th window and \eta(t_k) is given by (2.9).

