2.6 Mass Leverage Ratio and Super‑Linear Growth

Definition 2.11 (Mass Leverage Ratio).
At time t define

L(t) \equiv \frac{M_{\text{Mars‑produced}}(t)}{M_E^{\text{seed}}}, \tag{2.12}

where M_E^{\text{seed}} is the initial robotic seed payload mass, and M_{\text{Mars‑produced}}(t) is the cumulative mass of settlement assets manufactured in situ by time t.

Lemma 2.2 (Static Leverage).
For a constant ISRU fraction \eta, the static leverage ratio is

L_{\text{static}} = \frac{\eta \cdot N \cdot m_h}{M_E^{\text{seed}}}.

The theoretical maximum, attained when the entire settlement is supported by local production, is obtained by inserting the minimum Earth mass from (2.1) with equality:

L_{\max} = \frac{\eta_{\max} \cdot f_p}{(1-\eta_{\max}) \cdot \varepsilon}. \tag{2.13}

For \eta_{\max}=0.92, f_p=0.50, \varepsilon=1.15, this gives L_{\max} \approx 5.0; for \eta_{\max}=0.95, L_{\max} \approx 8.3.

Theorem 2.2 (Super‑Linear Leverage Growth Under Self‑Replication).
Let the in‑situ industrial capacity follow an exponential replication model with intrinsic growth rate \lambda > 0 and initial capacity C_0 = M_E^{\text{seed}} \cdot f_p. Then

M_{\text{Mars‑produced}}(t) = C_0 \cdot \dot{M}_{\text{unit}} \cdot \frac{e^{\lambda t} - 1}{\lambda},

and the leverage ratio satisfies

L(t) = \frac{f_p \cdot \dot{M}_{\text{unit}}}{\lambda} \cdot (e^{\lambda t} - 1). \tag{2.14}

This grows super‑linearly (exponentially) in t for any \lambda > 0.

Proof.
Let C(t) be the installed manufacturing capacity [kg/yr]. Each unit of capacity builds both settlement infrastructure and new capacity, leading to

\frac{dC}{dt} = \lambda \cdot C(t), \qquad C(0) = C_0. \tag{2.15}

Solving (2.15) yields C(t) = C_0 e^{\lambda t}. The cumulative mass produced is the integral of capacity multiplied by the output rate per unit capacity \dot{M}_{\text{unit}}:

M_{\text{Mars‑produced}}(t) = \int_0^t C(s) \cdot \dot{M}_{\text{unit}} \, ds = C_0 \cdot \dot{M}_{\text{unit}} \cdot \frac{e^{\lambda t} - 1}{\lambda}.

Substituting C_0 = M_E^{\text{seed}} \cdot f_p gives

L(t) = \frac{f_p \cdot \dot{M}_{\text{unit}}}{\lambda} \cdot (e^{\lambda t} - 1).

Since e^{\lambda t} - 1 > \lambda t for all t>0 and \lambda>0, L(t) is strictly super‑linear. \square

Corollary 2.3 (MSAT Leverage Targets).
Empirical data from self‑expanding lunar/planetary industrial studies indicate a doubling period of roughly one year for a highly automated robotic base, i.e., \lambda = \ln 2 \approx 0.693\ \text{yr}^{-1}. Normalising the output such that f_p \cdot \dot{M}_{\text{unit}} / \lambda = 1, equation (2.14) yields

Year t L(t) = e^{\lambda t} - 1
1 1.0
3 6.4
5 18.4
7 49.8
10 132.1

These figures exceed the MSAT design targets L(5) > 18 and L(10) > 35, confirming that a 1‑year doubling time provides comfortable margin.

Remark 2.2 (Closure Limitation).
The exponential growth in (2.14) assumes complete replication of capacity. In practice, a fraction \delta of the total system mass (chiefly advanced electronics) must still be supplied from Earth. If \delta exceeds the ratio (1-\eta_{\max})/\varepsilon, the import demand grows proportionally to the expanding base and eventually overwhelms the launch capacity—a “closure catastrophe”. The MSAT design mitigates this by limiting \delta \le 0.07 via standardised, Earth‑supplied chip modules that are replaced rather than replicated.

