At a landed payload capacity of 22–26\ \text{t} per heavy‑lift vehicle, this translates to approximately 230–272 lander‑equivalents, spread across 5–7 synodic windows. The initial seed payload of 22–26\ \text{t} thus provides \eta_0 \approx 0.05 of the eventual production capacity, in agreement with the logistic ramp.

Validation of Year‑1 ISRU throughput: for N=10 initial crew and \eta(1) = 0.113,

M_{\text{ISRU, yr1}} = \eta(1) \times N \times m_h = 0.113 \times 10 \times 325 \approx 367\ \text{t}.

Shielding regolith dominates this mass. Autonomous excavation rovers are projected to emplace 2–5\ \text{t/hr} each; a fleet of three units operating at 3\ \text{t/hr} for 6\,000\ \text{hr/yr} can move 54\,000\ \text{t/yr}, far exceeding the requirement. The bottleneck therefore lies not in excavation but in thermal processing for structural components and in AIADN coordination latency (Chapter 4).

