2.8 Design Implications and AIADN Coupling

The Bootstrap Inequality and the dynamic ISRU model impose four hard engineering constraints:

Constraint 2.1 (Shielding Dominance → ASR Priority).
Because m_{\text{shield}}/m_h \approx 0.82–0.93 and shielding is 100\% ISRU‑substitutable, regolith excavation throughput is the highest‑leverage single action to minimise M_E. The AIADN must allocate the highest task priority to Autonomous Shielding Rovers (ASR) during Years 0–2.

Constraint 2.2 (Structural Steel → Early Activation of ARC/AWR).
The remaining 8–18% of mass that requires thermal processing (smelting, sintering) must be initiated by Year 1–2 to avoid a bottleneck when crew size scales up.

Constraint 2.3 (Payload Efficiency → QSI‑1 Multi‑Function Design).
A 1\% absolute improvement in f_p reduces the required Earth mass by

\Delta M_E \approx \frac{N \cdot m_h \cdot (1-\eta) \cdot \varepsilon}{f_p^2} \cdot 0.01 \approx 120\ \text{t}

at the reference point. The QSI‑1 quadruped platform must therefore maximise task breadth per unit mass, eliminating all single‑purpose robots from the manifest.

Constraint 2.4 (Closure Fraction Cap).
To avoid the closure catastrophe, the non‑replicable mass fraction \delta must satisfy

\delta \le \frac{1 - \eta_{\max}}{\varepsilon} \approx \frac{0.08}{1.15} \approx 0.07,

i.e., at most 7\% of total installed system mass per replication cycle may consist of Earth‑manufactured electronics. This directly bounds the mass budget of AIADN chip modules.

