2.9 Summary

This chapter has established the core mathematical constraint for a bootstrapped Martian settlement.

· Theorem 2.1 provides the necessary condition M_E \ge K \cdot N \cdot (1-\eta), with the bootstrap constant K now correctly incorporating payload efficiency and overhead losses. The nominal value K \approx 748\ \text{t/person} (conservative K \approx 1000\ \text{t/person}) gives a transparent estimate of the required Earth launch mass for any crew size and ISRU fraction.
· Lemma 2.1 gives the closed‑form logistic trajectory \eta(t), replacing a static ISRU assumption with a time‑resolved model that enables synodic‑window import scheduling via the dynamic inequality (2.11).
· Theorem 2.2 proves that the Mass Leverage Ratio grows exponentially under self‑replication, validating the MSAT targets L(5) > 18 and L(10) > 35 as conservative for a 1‑year robotic doubling time.

