Appendix A. Detailed Derivation of Shielding Mass from First Principles

This appendix provides the step‑by‑step calculation of the per‑person regolith shielding mass, based on the most recent published measurements and Monte‑Carlo simulation data.

A.1 Galactic Cosmic Ray (GCR) Surface Dose

The unshielded effective dose equivalent rate at the Martian surface during solar minimum is taken from the Mars Science Laboratory’s Radiation Assessment Detector (RAD) data (Hassler et al., 2014). The measured mean absorbed dose rate is 0.21\ \text{mGy/day}, and the mean quality factor for the mixed GCR field is approximately 3.05, yielding a dose equivalent rate

\dot{D}_0 = 0.21 \times 3.05 \approx 0.64\ \text{mSv/day}.

Integrating over one Earth year gives

D_0 = 365.25 \times 0.64 \approx 234\ \text{mSv/yr}.

A conservative value of 240\ \text{mSv/yr} is adopted to account for solar minimum enhancements.

A.2 Attenuation by the Martian Atmosphere

The Martian atmosphere provides a column depth of approximately 18\ \text{g/cm}^2 of CO₂, which attenuates the primary GCR flux. However, the RAD measurement was taken after this atmospheric attenuation; therefore the dose of 240\ \text{mSv/yr} already includes the atmospheric shielding. The additional regolith overburden must reduce the dose from 240\ \text{mSv/yr} to below the regulatory limit D^* = 50\ \text{mSv/yr}.

A.3 Regolith Attenuation Curves

Monte‑Carlo simulations (HZETRN, MULASSIS/GEANT4) for Martian regolith show that the dose equivalent behind a regolith shield of areal density \Sigma (in g/cm²) is well approximated by

D(\Sigma) = D_0 \exp\left(-\Sigma / \lambda_1\right) + D_{\text{sec}} \left[1 - \exp\left(-\Sigma / \lambda_2\right)\right],

where \lambda_1 \approx 65\ \text{g/cm}^2 is the attenuation length for primary particles, and D_{\text{sec}} \approx 30\ \text{mSv/yr} is the asymptotic secondary particle dose (mainly neutrons) that cannot be eliminated by pure regolith.

Setting D(\Sigma) = 50\ \text{mSv/yr} and solving for \Sigma yields

\Sigma \approx 300\ \text{g/cm}^2.

A design value of \Sigma_s = 375\ \text{g/cm}^2 is chosen to provide a safety margin of about 25\% and to cover variations in regolith composition.

A.4 Conversion to Physical Thickness and Mass

The bulk density of compacted Martian regolith is taken as \rho_r = 1.5\ \text{g/cm}^3. Hence the required physical thickness is

d_s = \frac{\Sigma_s}{\rho_r} = \frac{375}{1.5} = 250\ \text{cm} = 2.5\ \text{m}.

The per‑person shielding mass is then

m_{\text{shield}} = \rho_r \cdot d_s \cdot A_p.

With A_p = 80\ \text{m}^2 (the effective footprint per person, including corridors and utility spaces), we obtain

m_{\text{shield}} = 1.5\ \text{t/m}^3 \times 2.5\ \text{m} \times 80\ \text{m}^2 = 300\ \text{t}.

A conservative upper bound of 350\ \text{t} corresponds to a 17\% safety margin on density and footprint.

