Appendix B. Sensitivity and Uncertainty Propagation for the Bootstrap Inequality

B.1 Partial Derivatives

The lower bound on the Earth‑launched mass is

M_E(N,\eta,f_p,\varepsilon) = \frac{N \cdot m_h \cdot (1-\eta) \cdot \varepsilon}{f_p}.

The local sensitivity coefficients (first derivatives) are:

\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial M_E}{\partial N}     &= \frac{m_h (1-\eta) \varepsilon}{f_p} = \frac{M_E}{N}, \\
\frac{\partial M_E}{\partial \eta}   &= -\frac{N m_h \varepsilon}{f_p} = -\frac{M_E}{1-\eta}, \\
\frac{\partial M_E}{\partial f_p}   &= -\frac{N m_h (1-\eta) \varepsilon}{f_p^2} = -\frac{M_E}{f_p}, \\
\frac{\partial M_E}{\partial \varepsilon} &= \frac{N m_h (1-\eta)}{f_p} = \frac{M_E}{\varepsilon}.
\end{aligned}

For the nominal reference point (N=100, \eta=0.92, f_p=0.50, \varepsilon=1.15), the numerical values are

\frac{\partial M_E}{\partial \eta} \approx -86\,250\ \text{t}, \qquad
\frac{\partial M_E}{\partial f_p} \approx -11\,968\ \text{t}, \qquad
\frac{\partial M_E}{\partial \varepsilon} \approx 5\,203\ \text{t}.

These magnitudes confirm that ISRU utilisation \eta is the dominant lever.

B.2 Variance Propagation Formula

If the input parameters are independent random variables with variances \sigma_\eta^2, \sigma_f^2, \sigma_\varepsilon^2, the variance of the required mass is approximated by the delta method:

\text{Var}(M_E) \approx \left(\frac{\partial M_E}{\partial \eta}\right)^2 \sigma_\eta^2
+ \left(\frac{\partial M_E}{\partial f_p}\right)^2 \sigma_{f_p}^2
+ \left(\frac{\partial M_E}{\partial \varepsilon}\right)^2 \sigma_\varepsilon^2.

Assuming \sigma_\eta = 0.02 (two percentage points), \sigma_{f_p} = 0.05, and \sigma_\varepsilon = 0.05, one obtains

\text{Var}(M_E) \approx (86\,250 \times 0.02)^2 + (11\,968 \times 0.05)^2 + (5\,203 \times 0.05)^2,

which yields a standard deviation of approximately 2\,200\ \text{t}. This is about 37\% of the nominal M_E bound, indicating the importance of reducing uncertainty in \eta through early robotic demonstrations.

