Appendix C. Calibration of the Logistic ISRU Ramp Parameter

The logistic growth rate \alpha in equation (2.8) is calibrated against the intended MSAT schedule. Given the target values \eta(1\,\text{yr}) \approx 0.10–0.15 and \eta(5\,\text{yr}) \approx 0.90–0.92, with \eta_0 = 0.05 and \eta_{\max} = 0.92, the solving of (2.9) at t=5 yields

0.897 = \frac{0.92}{1 + \left(\frac{0.87}{0.05}\right) e^{-5\alpha}}.

Rearranging:

1 + 17.4\, e^{-5\alpha} = \frac{0.92}{0.897} \approx 1.0257 \quad \Rightarrow \quad e^{-5\alpha} \approx \frac{0.0257}{17.4} \approx 0.001477.

Thus

-5\alpha \approx \ln(0.001477) \approx -6.517 \quad \Rightarrow \quad \alpha \approx 1.303\ \text{yr}^{-1}.

However, this value would imply an ISRU doubling time of \ln 2 / \alpha \approx 0.53\ \text{yr}, which is faster than the empirical 1‑year doubling time used in the literature for self‑replicating robotic bases. The MSAT simulations (Chapter 5) instead derive \alpha \approx 0.85\ \text{yr}^{-1} from detailed swarm throughput models, with the slightly slower ramp compensated by higher initial seed capacity and parallel swarm expansion. The value \alpha = 0.85\ \text{yr}^{-1} is adopted as the engineering baseline.

C.1 Sensitivity to \alpha

The time required to achieve a given ISRU fraction is inversely proportional to \alpha. The effect on the integrated mass budget (2.11) is nonlinear; a 10\% reduction in \alpha can increase the early import demand by about 15–20\% because the settlement must rely on Earth‑sourced shielding for a longer initial period.

